f £27,500, but the modelled retirement expenditure of £2,000 per month (£24,000 per annum) is below the stated minimum target of £25,000.","Fact Find [P10] records 'current pension value is circa £300,000' but the canonical model shows total existing arrangements of £327,945.44 (Quilter £260,000 + True Potential £46,672.80 + Nest £21,272.64). The discrepancy of £27,945 (9.3% higher) is not reconciled.","The inheritance amount is stated in the report's cashflow assumptions as £350,000 received next year, but Meeting Summary [P11] records 'at least £150,000 as your share' from mother's estate valued at £1.4 million, with timeline uncertain due to legal proceedings. These are materially different figures and certainty levels."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The requirement tests whether the target retirement income stated in the report is consistent with evidence and with the figures the plan is modelled on. Three distinct consistency questions arise:\n\n1. **Consistency between stated target and modelled expenditure**: The report states a target income of £25,000–£30,000 annually (midpoint £27,500). The cashflow analysis models retirement expenditure at £2,000 net per month, or £24,000 per annum. This is below the stated minimum target. However, the stated target is described as income to be 'drawn', and the modelled expenditure of £2,000 monthly may represent only the net personal share after State Pension begins at 67, not the full drawdown during the 60–67 window when the stated £25,000–£30,000 target applies. The extraction notes an ambiguity: pre-retirement expenditure is £2,237 per month, but retirement expenditure assumptions state £2,000 net per month. The report does not explain whether the £2,000 figure is net of the State Pension or an absolute expenditure level. This ambiguity prevents full reconciliation. The stated target income range of £25,000–£30,000 is evidenced in [P11] Meeting Summary verbatim, so the target itself is supported. The question is whether the modelled assumptions would achieve it.\n\n2. **Consistency of inheritance assumptions**: The report's cashflow assumptions state an inheritance of £350,000 received next year. Meeting Summary [P11] records the client expects 'at least £150,000 as your share' from an estate valued at £1.4 million, with timeline uncertain. Fact Find [P10] notes 'She has a future inheritance of £150,000 and this will settle remainder of her mortgage liability, meaning she will go into retirement with no debts.' The report has inflated this from £150,000 to £350,000 without explanation and assigned a certain timing ('next year') to an event described as subject to legal proceedings with uncertain timing. This is a material factual inconsistency. The report's modelling assumes £350,000; the evidence supports only £150,000 with uncertain timing.\n\n3. **Consistency of pension fund value**: The extraction notes a discrepancy between Fact Find [P10] ('current pension value is circa £300,000') and the canonical model's sum of £327,945.44. The difference of £27,945 (9.3%) is unexplained. The three arrangements sum to £327,945.44 per the figures checked in code. This may reflect the date of the Fact Find versus the valuation dates of the pension statements, but the inconsistency is not resolved in the evidence provided.\n\n4. **Basis of income target**: The report states the target income as 'Net' basis. The modelled figures in the assumptions (£2,237 pre-retirement, £2,000 retirement) are also net. The stated range £25,000–£30,000 in [P11] does not specify gross or net, but the context ('annual pension of £25,000 to £30,000') and the client's role as an accountant (noted in [P11]) suggests understanding of net concepts. The basis consistency appears sound, but the report's explicit statement of 'Net' basis for the £27,500 midpoint is not evidenced in those words in the case file — it is the report's interpretation of the client's stated range.\n\nThe extraction notes an internal inconsistency in the report: 'Client objectives state desire to retire at 60 with annual income of £25,000–£30,000 and explicit preference for flexible income over guaranteed income. Risk profile is assessed as 7/10 (Growth). Cashflow analysis shows assets would be sufficient even in stress scenarios including 6% inflation and 2007/8-style market crash. However, the incompatibility between a growth-oriented risk profile and the relatively modest income target (£25k–£30k on projected assets) is not explicitly addressed; the high risk profile appears suited to capital accumulation rather than the stated income objectives.' This tension is not resolved in the report's text and represents a lack of coherence between objectives and strategy, but it is not a factual inconsistency between report and file.\n\nGuard application: The target income is stated in today's terms (£25,000–£30,000) and the modelled figures assume 2% inflation. The report should reconcile these using stated escalation. The modelled retirement expenditure of £2,000 per month beginning at age 60 would escalate at 2% p.a., reaching approximately £2,219 per month by age 67 (7 years × 2% = 14% cumulative, or £2,000 × 1.1486 ≈ £2,297 at year 7). Over the 60–67 window this would average higher than £2,000. However, the report does not show this calculation, leaving the comparison incomplete.\n\nConclusion on comparability: The target income is evidenced and stated clearly in [P11]. The modelled assumptions exist but the basis of the retirement expenditure assumption (whether it is the full income need or only the personal net share, and how it escalates) is not fully disclosed. The inheritance amount is materially different (£350,000 assumed vs. £150,000 evidenced), which affects the sufficiency of the plan but not the consistency of the stated income target itself. The income target of £25,000–£30,000 is supported by evidence; the plan's assumptions about how to deliver it contain inconsistencies and unexplained gaps in logic, particularly around the £2,000 retirement expenditure and the £350,000 inheritance.","citations":[{"passageId":"P11","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"You aim to draw an annual pension of £25,000 to £30,000 until your state pension begins at 67.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"You plan to retire at the age of 60.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"You prefer a drawdown approach to receive a monthly income.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"In addition to your salary, you anticipate a significant inheritance from your late mother's property, which is valued at around £1.4 million. You expect to receive at least £150,000 as your share, although the timeline for this inheritance is uncertain due to ongoing legal proceedings involving your mother's ex-partner.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"She has a future inheritance of £150,000 and this will settle remainder of her mortgage liability, meaning she will go into retirement with no debts.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Current pension value is circa £300,000 and with the view to return to part time work in the near future the reliance on the pension will drop as the immediate need at present will temporary.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Based on the above her capacity for loss is medium to high, however i feel this is leaning more to medium as if the withdrawals continue at the projected rate of £1,900 per month this will reduce longevity.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Data quality"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

