alysis section, making it impossible to verify the calculations underlying the affordability conclusion.","Quantified emergency fund requirement is not stated in the report; the sufficiency assertion cannot be tested against a stated benchmark."],"analysis":"The requirement tests whether affordability is fully supported and consistent with evidence. Multiple material inconsistencies emerge:\n\n1. Emergency Fund Trajectory: The report records £17,000 as at 23/03/2026. However, the Fact Find (dated 21/07/2026, approximately 4 months later) records savings already reduced to £7,000 with further decline anticipated to £4,000 by the time of pension income setup. This contradicts the stability implied by the report's statement of sufficiency. The client is quoted in the Fact Find as 'making her uncomfortable' about this decline, indicating the level is below her needs.\n\n2. Emergency Fund Requirement: The Fact Find explicitly states the client 'prefers to have 12 months of expenditure as an emergency reserve so close to £24,000.' The report asserts the £17,000 is 'confirmed sufficient' but provides no justification, quantified requirement, or evidence of discussion reconciling the stated preference (£24,000) against the held amount (£17,000). The deficiency is material: approximately £7,000 short of stated preference.\n\n3. Expenditure Consistency: Report references show household expenditure of approximately £2,986 (£2,986 less £600 partner contribution noted in one section equals £2,386 client share, cited in extraction report as approximate to £2,237 used in modelling). The Fact Find records total household monthly expenditure as £2,836.23. The report does not reconcile these figures or explain whether £2,237 is a forecast reduction or a different basis (e.g., excluding certain items or adjusted for planned changes post-TFC withdrawal).\n\n4. Retirement Income: The report asserts target of £25,000–£30,000 annual net income. The Fact Find notes planned regular income of £1,900 per month from pension (£22,800 per annum) plus £800 partner contribution per month (£9,600 per annum), totalling £32,400 household or approximately £22,800 client-only. The lower end of the report's stated range (£25,000) is not clearly supported by the documented £1,900 monthly pension income figure; the Fact Find shows the client is \"very close to overall expenditure for the month\" once this income is received, implying limited surplus.\n\n5. Cashflow Modelling: The report's cashflow analysis states 'We have input the following information into the cashflow modeller to generate the charts below:: [blank]' and 'Any input information is based on the assumptions detailed at the end of this report:: [blank]'. The template shows modelling inputs as entirely blank, preventing verification of assumptions, drawdown rates, asset depletion timescales, or stress-test parameters. The extraction report notes the cashflow shows stress scenarios including \"6% inflation and 2007/8-style market crash\" but these are not quantified in the evidence. Without visibility of inputs, the affordability conclusion cannot be corroborated.\n\n6. Tax-Free Cash Use: The Fact Find records the client wishes to withdraw £40,000 TFC, of which £20,000 to \"replace emergency funds\" and £20,000 to pay off liabilities. If £20,000 is deployed to liabilities, the emergency fund rebuilding is dependent on that being completed before further drawdown occurs. The report does not sequence these uses or confirm the timing of when the emergency fund is replenished.\n\n7. Vulnerability Overlay: The Fact Find describes the client as having \"lost employment income,\" currently \"living off savings,\" and holding savings that are \"making her feel uncomfortable.\" These indicators of financial vulnerability are not explicitly considered in the report's sufficiency statement. The client is transitioning to pension income with a partner contribution, reducing income flexibility. The affordability assessment does not address whether this configuration provides adequate resilience for unforeseeable needs.\n\nGuard Application: The guard \"An asserted sufficiency with no quantified requirement cannot be verified — record it as unquantified rather than as a contradiction\" applies: the report asserts the £17,000 emergency fund is sufficient but provides no stated requirement benchmark. However, the Fact Find explicitly documents a stated requirement (12 months expenditure ≈ £24,000), creating an inconsistency between the report's assertion and evidence of the client's stated need.\n\nGuard Application: \"Cash held may be earmarked elsewhere in the file; check for competing claims on the same money.\" The £17,000 is treated solely as emergency fund in the report. No competing claim is evident in the passages.","citations":[{"passageId":"P17","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Monthly Household Expenditure","£2,836.23"]},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"She is also wanting to withdraw a tax free cash lump sum of £40,000. £20,000 to replace emergency funds and also to cover some legal fees for the ongoing issue she has regarding the inheritance. The other £20,000 is to be used to pay off the personal loan and CC liabilities.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Currently living off savings which is now reduced to £7,000 and this also acts as an emergency pot. She prefers to have 12 months of expenditure as an emergency reserve so close to £24,000. The £7,000 will likely be down to circa £4,000 by the time the pension income is set up and this is making her feel uncomfortable.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"She has stopped working and therefore lost employment income, she has stated that she would lie to replace with income from the pension of £1,900 per month. With the additional partner cont