ether it will be eliminated before retirement, or how contributions to the recommended pension can be sustained given this deficit. No cashflow model reconciling current position with future affordability is presented.","Emergency fund: The file contains no quantified emergency fund or liquid reserves figure. The report asserts that sustainability is assessed but provides no evidence of emergency reserves or liquid assets available to cover the income-expenditure gap or contingencies."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Cashflow model or affordability assessment that reconciles the current monthly deficit of -£2,036.23 with the ability to sustain contributions and withdrawals post-2026.","Quantified emergency fund or liquid reserves available to the client.","Clarification of the relationship between the stated retirement age (60), the withdrawal start date (2026-06-24), and the access-need date (2031-03-24).","Explanation of how the current income-expenditure deficit is funded and when/whether it will resolve.","Detail of ongoing contribution commitments and their sustainability given the evidenced deficit.","Reconciliation between the client's stated need for capital access (if any) and the wrapper's access restrictions."],"analysis":"The comparison can be performed in part, but material gaps prevent full assessment of affordability and liquidity fit. The report asserts the recommended wrapper is a Personal Pension with UFPLS withdrawals. Aviva product literature [P2, P13, P20] confirms that Personal Pensions permit UFPLS access from minimum pension age (currently 55). At the client's apparent age (not explicitly stated but withdrawal start in 2026 and retirement at 60 suggest current age circa 35–40), minimum pension age restrictions are not a barrier; the client will be 55+ by 2026 when withdrawals are to commence. The product wrapper itself does not lock capital beyond stated access dates. However, three material inconsistencies undermine the affordability finding: (1) The retirement age of 60 does not align with the withdrawal start date of 2026. If the client is currently under 50, age 60 is still 10+ years away, but withdrawals are to start in circa 4 years. This suggests either early access before stated retirement age or an error in one of these figures. The report does not explain this discrepancy. (2) The liquidityAndAccessNeeds date of 2031-03-24 is stated but the withdrawal schedule commences 2026-06-24, approximately 5 years earlier. The file does not explain whether 2031 is a secondary access need or whether the 2026 date is provisional. This ambiguity undermines confidence that affordability has been properly assessed against actual needs. (3) The Fact Find shows a severe current income-expenditure deficit (monthly disposable income of -£2,036.23). The report does not evidence how this deficit is currently met, whether it will resolve before contributions are to commence, or how contributions will be affordable. The internal note on ambiguities flags that monthly expenditure figures differ (£2,986 household vs £2,237 personal), but even the lower figure of £2,237 exceeds the net income of £800 monthly, leaving a deficit of circa £1,437. No cashflow or affordability assessment in the file reconciles this gap with the recommendation. (4) No emergency fund is quantified. The requirement to assess financial resilience includes addressing whether liquid reserves sufficient to cover the current deficit and contingencies exist. The file is silent on this. (5) The report states sustainability of withdrawals is assessed (isSustainabilityAssessed = true) but the file contains no explicit cashflow model, stress test, or sustainability narrative supporting this claim. The absence of such evidence, combined with the unexplained income-expenditure deficit and the discrepancies in dates, means the affordability claim is not supported by evidence in the file. This is a Potential Concern under the decision logic: 'A