{"groupId":"CHK-007","requirement":"[G7.5] Benefit versus cost — fair value. Limb: Consistency. Where costs, charges and the value of recommendations are referenced within the Suitability Report, does the Suitability Report disclose all material charges relevant to the recommendation, including applicable initial, ongoing, product, platform, investment and transaction costs, and where higher costs arise, does the report clearly explain the benefits expected to be received by the client in exchange for those costs and are they supported by and consistent with the evidence provided?","reportSays":"The suitability report asserts multiple cost benefits: (1) 'Lower overall cost structure compared to the Quilter plan' [P1]; (2) Platform charges reduced from 0.27% to 0.19%, saving £208 per annum based on estimated fund value [P1]; (3) For True Potential: platform charges reduced from 0.40% to 0.21%, saving £88.68 per annum [P1]; (4) For True Potential: fund charges reduced from 0.58% to 0.20%, saving £177.35 per annum [P1]; (5) Benefits cited include active monitoring, fund alignment with risk level, wider investment options, flexible retirement options, and online platform access [P1]. The report states the CIP approach offers 'Cost Efficiency' balancing performance and cost while optimising value [P3]. No aggregated all-in-cost comparison is presented in the report. The report does not disclose the ongoing adviser charges (0.50% for True Potential per the extracted inconsistencies) in the cost comparison tables. The true cost comparison excludes the adviser's ongoing remuneration from the switching analysis, presenting only platform and fund charges as the basis for the cost-savings claim.","fileSays":"Evidence shows existing arrangements total £327,945.44 across three pensions (Quilter £260,000.00 at 0.27% platform charge = £208 annual; True Potential £46,672.80 with 0.40% platform + 0.50% ongoing advice + 0.58% fund charges totalling 1.48%; Nest £21,272.64 at 0.30% plan charge + 1.8% contribution charge = 2.1% total). Switching analysis documents [P12, P14, P15, P16] show projected fund values at retirement (age 87): Aviva Pension Portfolio at 2.94% growth projects to £151,000 (inflation-adjusted) versus existing plan £134,612.81; RIY shown as 0.4% [P14]. The arithmetic check from the canonical model reveals: EA1 (Quilter) annual charge £208 at 0.27% implies fund value £77,037.04, which matches no single arrangement; Solution total ongoing £1,278.99 at 0.39% implies fund value £327,946.15 (matches total of all arrangements); EA2 (True Potential) existing annual charge £457.39 at 0.98% implies value £46,672.45 (matches True Potential value); EA3 (Nest) existing annual charge £63.82 at 0.30% implies value £21,273.33 (matches Nest value). The report claims platform charges for Quilter will reduce to 0.19% but provides no independent documentation confirming the receiving scheme's platform charges. Fund literature shows True Potential funds with OCF charges (e.g., Goldman Sachs Balanced at 0.87% [P5]). Proposed Aviva funds show ongoing costs of 0.2154% weighted average [P11].","discrepancies":["Report states platform charge saving of £208 per annum for Quilter based on reducing from 0.27% to 0.19%, but the £208 figure at 0.27% implies a fund value of £77,037.04, not the £260,000 Quilter value stated in existing arrangements","Report claims True Potential platform charge saving of £88.68 (from 0.40% to 0.21%), but £88.68 at 0.40% implies a fund value of £22,170, not the £46,672.80 stated for True Potential","Report claims True Potential fund charge saving of £177.35 (from 0.58% to 0.20%), but £177.35 at 0.58% implies a fund value of £30,577, not £46,672.80","True Potential existing charges stated in arrangements table as 0.40% + 0.50% + 0.58% = 1.48%, but replacement analysis shows current recurring charge as 0.98%, excluding the 0.50% ongoing advice charge from the switching comparison","Report does not disclose that it is excluding 