{"groupId":"CHK-008","requirement":"[G8.1] Material disadvantages, risk factors and limitations of the recommendation: Consistency. Does the Suitability Report fairly, clearly, accurately and prominently explain all material disadvantages, risks, limitations, tax consequences, potential loss scenarios and consumer harms associated with the recommendations (including pension withdrawals and pension transfers where relevant), and are these explanations consistent with the supporting evidence provided, and does the report adequately disclose any material information, risks or limitations identified elsewhere in the supporting evidence?","reportSays":"The report states the following material disadvantages and limitations: (1) Access delay: \"it will usually take 10-15 working days for administration, disinvestment and banking processes to complete\" [I, p.12, 14]. (2) Lower absolute performance: \"The recommended funds, being more balanced and in line with your risk tolerance, has understandably lagged behind the higher-risk funds but has demonstrated strong risk-adjusted performance within its category\" [I, p.20]. (3) Market timing risk: \"if the market rises in value between the date of realising your existing funds and the purchase of your new funds, you could be financially disadvantaged\" [I, p.12]. (4) No guarantee of outperformance: \"There is no guarantee the return on the new investment will be greater than that of your existing arrangement for these funds\" [I, p.12]. (5) Higher charges: \"The annual charges for the recommend plan are higher at 0.39% compared to 0.30%, representing an increase of £19.15 per annum, based on the current values\" [I, p.12]. (6) Projection limitations: \"even a small variance between the assumptions used and actual experience can result in a marked difference in the outcome when compounded over a long period of time\" [I, p.22]. (7) Long-term care not considered: \"the chart does not consider any potential long-term care costs which could occur in later life\" [I, p.22]. (8) Cancellation limitation: \"any previous provider is not obliged to take the plan back\" [I, p.26]. The report also discloses tax treatment is based on current HMRC practice which may change [I, p.20].","fileSays":"The Aviva Pension Key Features Document [P2, P4, P11, P12] identifies material risks and disadvantages applicable to the recommended product including: (1) Investment and market risks generally: \"The value of your pension pot and the retirement benefits it can provide may go down as well as up. You could get back less than has been invested\" [P5]. (2) Small company and overseas investment risks: \"Small company investments may take longer to sell... The value of any overseas investments will be influenced by the rate of exchange\" [P2]. (3) Property investment risks: \"If the underlying investments include property, it may take some time before we can sell your investment\" [P2]. (4) Market timing risk on transfer: \"As a result of trading practices, there's a possibility that the price of investments may fall or rise in the period between us receiving your instructions and the time of the transaction\" [P2]. (5) Illiquid assets risk: \"It may not be easy to sell investments such as commercial property\" [P2]. (6) Fixed-term product restrictions: \"If you invest in some products with a fixed term, then you may not be able to access the monies until the end of that term, and even where you can, you may receive back significantly less than you invested\" [P2]. (7) Income drawdown specific risks: \"Your investments need to grow to compensate for the income you withdraw. If that doesn't happen, the income you take will deplete your pension fund. Especially if you choose to take a high level of income\" [P2]. (8) Means-tested benefits impact: \"The income you take may have an impact on any means-tested benefits you're receiving\" [P2]. (9) Transfer of pension rights: \"If you're transferring benefits from an older company pension scheme it could be a Defined Benefit Pension and you may be losing valuable rights or benefits within the scheme such as a guaranteed income based on your final salary\" [P12]. (10) Structured products restrictions: \"It's not possible to transfer structured products either into or out of your Pension Portfolio\" [P11]. (11) Delays in investment execution: \"For Direct Debit and Immediate electronic Payments funds will clear within three Business Days of the crediting of monies to Your Cash Account to be invested\" [P3]; and separately administration processing and unit conversion delays during transfers [P23]. (12) Tax treatment subject to change: \"The current favourable tax treatment of the Pension Portfolio may change if tax rules change\" [P4].","discrepancies":["The report does not identify or discuss the risk that existing defined benefit pension rights or guaranteed income protections may be lost through the transfer, despite this being material for pension transfers and appearing prominently in the Aviva product literature [P12: 'you may be losing valuable rights or benefits within the scheme such as a guaranteed income based on your final salary']","The report does not identify the risk that income drawdown may deplete the pension fund if investment growth does not keep pace with withdrawals, despite this being material to the client's stated objective of flexible income and appearing in product literature [P2: 'Your investments need to grow to compensate for the income you withdraw. If that doesn't happen, the income you take will deplete your pension fund']","The report does not disclose the risk that means-tested benefits may be affected by pension income withdrawals, despite this being material to retirement planning and appearing in product literature [P2: 'The income you take may have an impact on any means-tested benefits you're receiving']","The report does not address small-cap or overseas investment currency risks, despite these being relevant to balanced growth funds and identified in product literature [P2]","The report does not identify illiquidity risks associated with property investments, despite these being relevant to the fund composition and appearing in product literature [P2]","The higher charges disadvantage stated in the report (0.39% vs 0.30%, £19.15 per annum) is calculated only on Nest's current value (£21,272.64) and appears incomplete; the analysis identifies three separate existing arrangements with differing charge bases, but the comparison does not account for the aggregate position or clarify which arrangement's charges are being compared","The report discloses a three-day settlement delay for new investments [implicit in 10-15 working days] but product literature separately identifies a three-day delay for cash clearance [P3] plus the time for trading, unit conversion, and disinvestment timing risks—these are multiple overlapping sources of access delay not fully synthesized in the disadvantages disclosure","The access delay disadvantage (10-15 working days) is stated generically without specifying whether this applies uniformly to all three arrangements being switched or whether it represents the worst-case timing"],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["The canonical model does not include guaranteed annual amounts or guaranteed payout periods for the three existing arrangements (Quilter, True Potential, Nest), listed as expectedButAbsent, which means the report's failure to disclose the loss of any defined benefit or guaranteed income protections cannot be fully assessed against the actual existing benefit structure","The basis of the 0.30% 'existing' charge used in the higher charges disadvantage is ambiguous—the underlying arrangements table shows 0.27% for Quilter, 0.98% for True Potential, and 0.30% for Nest, but it is unclear whether the comparison is against Nest only or whether it should account for consolidation effects","Detailed fund composition and exposure to small-cap, overseas, or property investments in both existing and recommended portfolios is not provided, limiting assessment of whether specific product-literature risks are material to this client's funds","Confirmation of whether any existing arrangement includes a defined benefit pension element is absent from the case file, limiting assessment of whether the defined benefit transfer warning in product literature is material"],"analysis":"The report discloses several material disadvantages of the recommendation and contains statements that are broadly consistent with the Aviva product literature. However, there are multiple material risks identified in the product literature and applicable to pension transfers that the report does not address. Most significantly: (1) The defined benefit pension transfer warning (loss of guaranteed income) is a FCA-mandated disclosure for pension transfers and appears prominently in the Aviva product literature [P12]. The extraction metadata indicates that guaranteed annual amounts for existing arrangements are not present in the report ('NotPresentInDocument'), suggesting the existing benefits were not analysed for defined benefit features. This omission is particularly material because the regulatory basis for this check includes COBS 9.4.7R and PRIN 2A.5, which require fair disclosure of what the client is giving up. (2) The income drawdown depletion risk [P2] is material to this client because the stated objectives explicitly include preference for 'flexible income over guaranteed income' and a target retirement income of £25,000–£30,000. The report does not warn that if investment returns do not keep pace with drawdowns, the fund will be depleted—this is the inverse of guaranteed income and directly relevant to the suitability of the recommendation. (3) The means-tested benefits risk [P2] is omitted from the report. This is a financial planning risk that should have been addressed, especially where the stated objectives mention flexibility and income planning. (4) The higher charges disadvantage is stated as 0.39% vs 0.30% on £19.15 per annum. The derivation shows this is calculated as £21,272.64 × 0.09% (Nest's value only), meaning the comparison is against Nest's 0.30% charge rate. However, the three existing arrangements have differing charge bases: Quilter 0.27%, True Potential 0.98%, and Nest 0.30%. The report's statement that charges increase from 0.30% to 0.39% is incomplete and potentially misleading if it implies this applies uniformly across the consolidation, when in fact True Potential's charges are 0.98% and the consolidated rate of 0.39% may represent an improvement for True Potential while a cost for Quilter and Nest. The charge analysis lacks transparency about which existing arrangement's rate is being used as the baseline and why. (5) The access delay (10-15 working days) is stated generically. The Aviva product literature identifies: a three-day clearing period for cash [P3], plus trading settlement periods, plus unit conversion delays during transfers [P23]. These are presented as a single disadvantage without decomposing the components or clarifying whether the 10-15 day estimate includes all of these or applies consistently to ad hoc withdrawals versus new transfers. (6) Specific product risks from the Aviva KFD—small-cap illiquidity, currency risk, property investment illiquidity, fixed-term product restrictions, and structured product transfer restrictions [P2, P11]—are not addressed in the report. While not every risk in product literature need be repeated if it is not material to this client's fund selection, the report provides no evidence that it has considered and concluded these are immaterial. The lack of detail on fund composition makes this assessment impossible. (7) The Tax Treatment section [I, p.20] states 'Levels and basis of tax relief are subject to change' but does not address the Aviva platform-level tax treatment risk that 'The current favourable tax treatment of the Pension Portfolio may change if tax rules change' [P4]. These are overlapping but distinct risks. The report's language is generic; Aviva's is specific to pension tax status. Consistency is achieved but specificity is limited. (8) The Cancellation limitation is stated [I, p.26]. This is consistent with the product literature framework (Aviva T&C addresses cancellation) [P3, P11], though the exact mechanics are not detailed in the passages provided. (9) Projection limitations are stated in generic form [I, p.22] and are consistent with standard industry practice and Aviva KFD disclosure [P2] that projections are not guaranteed. The report's use of cashflow modelling to project retirement sufficiency is sound in principle, but the disclosure of projection uncertainty could be more prominent. Overall, the report presents selected disadvantages but omits material risks that are either (a) FCA-mandated disclosures for pension transfers (defined benefit warning, loss of protections), (b) directly material to this client's stated objectives (income drawdown depletion risk, means-tested benefits impact), or (c) product-specific risks in the recommended fund literature (small-cap, currency, property, fixed-term, and structured product restrictions). The higher charges disadvantage is stated but the basis is incomplete and potentially misleading when viewed against the full suite of existing arrangements. The report's disclosures are not false, but they are incomplete and lack the specificity and synthesis that would constitute fair, clear and prominent explanation of all material disadvantages.","citations":[{"passageId":"P2","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"Your investments need to grow to compensate for the income you withdraw. If that doesn't happen, the income you take will deplete your pension fund. Especially if you choose to take a high level of income.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Aviva Pensio